  <h1>What Is an LFIA Reader?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> An LFIA reader is an instrument that measures the signal of a lateral-flow test strip — typically fluorescence — and converts it into a numeric (quantitative or semi-quantitative) result. It replaces naked-eye line reading with objective, instrument-assisted detection, enabling concentration output that a visual colloidal-gold strip cannot provide.</p>

  <h2>What a reader does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Excites a fluorescent label on the test and control lines.</li>
    <li>Measures the emitted signal intensity.</li>
    <li>Converts intensity to a concentration via a stored calibration curve.</li>
    <li>Displays a numeric result, reducing subjective line interpretation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fluorescence vs colloidal gold</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Colloidal gold (visual)</th><th>Fluorescence (reader)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Readout</th><td>Naked eye (qualitative)</td><td>Reader (quantitative)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Equipment</th><td>None</td><td>Fluorescence analyzer</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Output</th><td>Positive / negative</td><td>Concentration</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Best for</th><td>Screening, field use</td><td>Quantitative markers, objective results</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When fluorescence replaces colloidal gold</h2>
  <p>Choose fluorescence (reader-based) when:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You need a <strong>concentration</strong>, not just presence/absence (e.g., CRP, troponin, PCT).</li>
    <li>You need <strong>objective, reproducible</strong> results across operators.</li>
    <li>You need <strong>higher sensitivity</strong> than a visual line can reliably provide.</li>
    <li>You are doing <strong>serial monitoring</strong> where a trend matters.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Choose colloidal gold when cost, portability and instrument-free operation matter most — screening and field settings.</p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Readers add instrument cost, calibration and QC requirements.</li>
    <li>Fluorescence improves quantification and objectivity but does not remove assay-development fundamentals (antibody quality, conjugate stability).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the difference between an LFIA reader and a visual test?</strong> A reader measures a fluorescent signal and outputs a number; a visual test relies on a naked-eye line.</p>
    <p><strong>When should I use fluorescence instead of colloidal gold?</strong> When you need quantitative results, higher sensitivity or objective interpretation.</p>
    <p><strong>Is a reader-based test more accurate?</strong> It is more objective and quantitative; accuracy still depends on the underlying assay.</p>
  </div>

